# Film Stock and Bases
#
# Motion-picture film as a carrier: celluloid roll film, the 35 mm gauge, narrower amateur gauges, and the slow move from flammable nitrate to acetate safety film to polyester.

#>media_carriers_film_stock
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!media_carriers
#:form #!timeline
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk
#:era #!industrial

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Film Stock and Bases"
 ~#claim: "Film moved from nitrate, which burns, to triacetate, which turns to vinegar, to polyester, which is stable; Kodak moved camera and print stocks to triacetate between its 1948 announcement and the 1952 completion."
 ~#proof: "Coe and Enticknap for the gauge history; the NFPF guide and Reilly for the bases and their decay; the Kodak film chronology for the safety-base dates, with Wikipedia cross-checks."
 ~#door: "Read an old film edge for its base, then follow vinegar syndrome into the decay ledger."
 ~#audience: "film students, archivists, and families holding old home movies"
 continuity: "Film Stock and Bases | turns to vinegar"
 cold_open: "Film began as a base that burns, moved to one that turns to vinegar, and settled on polyester that lasts."
}

